\title{Bounded-Fiber Certificates for Rectangle Packing,\\Piercing, and Recursive Gap Constructions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 16, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a finite family $\mathcal R$ of axis-parallel rectangles, let $\nu(\mathcal R)$ be the maximum size of a pairwise disjoint subfamily and let $\tau(\mathcal R)$ be the minimum size of a point set meeting every rectangle. We isolate the finite counting certificates underlying a proposed $64$-rectangle violation of $\tau\leq2\nu-1$. A point-triangle-free family, in which no point belongs to three distinct indexed members, satisfies $|\mathcal R|\leq2|T|$ for every transversal $T$. A second certificate bounds a selected family by partitioning it into ordered slots of bounded capacity; four blocks with four capacity-one slots give a packing upper bound of $16$. Together with a displayed disjoint $16$-subfamily, these hypotheses certify $\nu=16$ and $\tau\geq32$, hence $2\nu-1=31<32\leq\tau$. We also solve $a_0=4$, $a_{r+1}=a_r^2$, obtaining $a_r=4^{2^r}$, and record $17891/8064<73/32$. The abstract certificates do not themselves supply coordinates, a $32$-point upper transversal, or a derivation of a linear-program optimum; they reduce those claims to sharply specified finite data.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Packing and piercing are dual themes in discrete geometry. Given a family of sets, packing seeks many mutually compatible objects, while piercing seeks few witnesses meeting all objects. For finite families of axis-parallel rectangles, the packing number $\nu$ is the largest cardinality of a pairwise disjoint subfamily, and the piercing number $\tau$ is the least number of points required to meet every rectangle.

The elementary inequality $\nu\leq\tau$ holds because one point cannot pierce two disjoint rectangles. A much stronger proposed universal bound was
\[
\tau(\mathcal R)\leq2\nu(\mathcal R)-1.
\]
At $\nu=16$, this predicts $\tau\leq31$. A family of $64$ rectangles with exact packing number $16$ and piercing number at least $32$ therefore gives a numerical violation.

The global estimates can be certified by two maps with bounded fibers. On the piercing side, map each rectangle to a chosen transversal point; a depth-two condition bounds every fiber by two. On the packing side, map each selected rectangle to an ordered block-slot cell; geometric incompatibility bounds every fiber by one. This viewpoint separates universal combinatorics from coordinate verification and extends to recursive horizontal--vertical constructions.

The results below are certificate implications. No coordinate realization follows merely from the counting statements. Likewise, the rational comparison involving $73/32$ is exact arithmetic, but establishing that number as an optimization value requires independent primal and dual certificates.

\section{Definitions}
Let $I$ be a finite index set and let $F_i\subseteq X$ for $i\in I$. Indexing matters: distinct indices may represent geometrically equal sets.

\begin{definition}
A finite set $T\subseteq X$ is a \emph{transversal} if $T\cap F_i\neq\varnothing$ for every $i\in I$. The piercing number is
\[
\tau(\mathcal F)=\min\{|T|:T\text{ is a transversal}\}.
\]
A subset $A\subseteq I$ indexes a \emph{pairwise disjoint subfamily} if $F_i\cap F_j=\varnothing$ for distinct $i,j\in A$. The packing number is
\[
\nu(\mathcal F)=\max\{|A|:A\text{ indexes a pairwise disjoint subfamily}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

For rectangles, $X=\mathbb R^2$ and $F_i=[\ell_i,r_i]\times[b_i,t_i]$ with $\ell_i\leq r_i$ and $b_i\leq t_i$.

\begin{definition}
A finite indexed family is \emph{point-triangle-free} if no point belongs to three distinct indexed members. Equivalently, if $x\in F_i\cap F_j\cap F_k$, then $i=j$, $i=k$, or $j=k$.
\end{definition}

For rectangles, pairwise-intersecting finite subfamilies share a point: the horizontal projections are pairwise-intersecting intervals and hence have a common coordinate, and similarly vertically. The counting theorem needs only the incidence definition above.

\begin{definition}
An $m$-slot certificate of capacity $b$ for a finite selection $A$ is a map $\sigma:A\to\{1,\ldots,m\}$ satisfying $|\sigma^{-1}(q)|\leq b$ for every slot $q$. A four-by-four capacity-one certificate assigns each selected member a pair in $\{1,2,3,4\}^2$, with no pair used twice.
\end{definition}

\section{The bounded-fiber principle}
\begin{lemma}[Bounded-fiber counting]
Let $A,Q$ be finite and $f:A\to Q$. If $|f^{-1}(q)|\leq c_q$ for every $q\in Q$, then
\[
|A|\leq\sum_{q\in Q}c_q.
\]
If $|Q|=m$ and every fiber has size at most $b$, then $|A|\leq mb$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The fibers form a disjoint partition of $A$, so
\[
|A|=\sum_{q\in Q}|f^{-1}(q)|\leq\sum_{q\in Q}c_q.
\]
The uniform statement takes $c_q=b$.
\end{proof}

\section{Piercing certificates from depth two}
\begin{theorem}[Triangle-Free Transversal Bound]
Let $\mathcal F=(F_i)_{i\in I}$ be a finite point-triangle-free indexed family in any ambient set. Every finite transversal $T$ satisfies
\[
|I|\leq2|T|.
\]
Consequently,
\[
\tau(\mathcal F)\geq\left\lceil\frac{|I|}{2}\right\rceil.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every $i$, choose $f(i)\in T\cap F_i$. If three distinct indices belonged to one fiber, their common image would lie in three distinct members, contrary to point-triangle-freeness. Every fiber has size at most two, and bounded-fiber counting gives $|I|\leq2|T|$. Minimize over transversals.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every transversal of a point-triangle-free family of $64$ indexed members has at least $32$ points.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The theorem gives $64\leq2|T|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The factor two is sharp under point-triangle-freeness alone: one point may belong to two members. Stronger bounds require additional hypotheses.
\end{remark}

\section{Packing certificates from ordered slots}
\begin{theorem}[Slot-Capacity Bound]
A finite selection assigned to $m$ slots, each receiving at most $b$ members, has size at most $mb$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply bounded-fiber counting to the slot map.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Four-by-Four Packing Bound]
Suppose every member of a finite selection $A$ is assigned a pair $(p(i),q(i))\in\{1,2,3,4\}^2$, and no pair is assigned to two members. Then $|A|\leq16$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The label space has $4\cdot4=16$ capacity-one cells.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact packing from a witness]
If every pairwise disjoint subfamily of $\mathcal F$ has a four-by-four capacity-one certificate and $\mathcal F$ contains a pairwise disjoint subfamily of size $16$, then $\nu(\mathcal F)=16$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The witness gives $\nu\geq16$, while the preceding theorem gives $\nu\leq16$.
\end{proof}

\section{The sixty-four-rectangle certificate}
\begin{theorem}[Certified Counterexample Bounds]
Let $\mathcal R$ be a family of $64$ axis-parallel rectangles. Suppose:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $\mathcal R$ is point-triangle-free;
\item $\mathcal R$ contains a specified pairwise disjoint subfamily of $16$ rectangles; and
\item every pairwise disjoint subfamily admits four block labels and four ordered slot labels, with every block-slot pair used at most once.
\end{enumerate}
Then
\[
\nu(\mathcal R)=16,
\qquad
\tau(\mathcal R)\geq32.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Exact packing follows from the witness and the four-by-four upper bound. The piercing estimate follows from the triangle-free transversal bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Numerical violation]
Under the hypotheses above,
\[
2\nu(\mathcal R)-1<\tau(\mathcal R).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Here $2\nu-1=2\cdot16-1=31$, whereas $\tau\geq32$.
\end{proof}

A lower bound on $\tau$ suffices for the strict violation. Exact equality $\tau=32$ would additionally require an explicit transversal of $32$ points.

\subsection{Coordinate certificates}
For $R_i=[\ell_i,r_i]\times[b_i,t_i]$, the hypotheses reduce to finite endpoint comparisons. Two rectangles are disjoint if one lies strictly left, right, above, or below the other. Three rectangles share a point exactly when the maximum of their horizontal lower endpoints does not exceed the minimum upper endpoint and the analogous vertical condition holds. A slot capacity can be verified by showing that any two equal-labeled rectangles intersect. A packing lower bound is certified by listing sixteen indices and checking pairwise disjointness. Exact piercing can be certified by listing thirty-two points and checking containment. Thus global geometry reduces to coordinates, labels, and order comparisons.

\section{Recursive horizontal--vertical composition}
\begin{theorem}[Squaring Recurrence Closed Form]
Let $(a_r)_{r\geq0}$ satisfy $a_0=4$ and $a_{r+1}=a_r^2$. Then
\[
a_r=4^{2^r}
\]
for every $r\geq0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $r$. The base case is $4=4^{2^0}$. If $a_r=4^{2^r}$, then
\[
a_{r+1}=a_r^2=(4^{2^r})^2=4^{2\cdot2^r}=4^{2^{r+1}}.
\]
\end{proof}
The first values are
\[
4,\quad16,\quad256,\quad65{,}536.
\]
The conclusion is conditional on the exact squaring law; a geometric construction must prove both matching lower and upper bounds at each composition step. Equality in a capacity-one bound forces saturation of every cell and may classify maximum packings recursively.

\section{Point relaxations and rational comparisons}
Assign a nonnegative variable $x_i$ to each rectangle and impose
\[
\sum_{i:p\in R_i}x_i\leq1
\]
for every point $p\in\mathbb R^2$, while maximizing $\sum_i x_i$. Binary feasible vectors encode disjoint subfamilies, whereas fractional vectors may have larger value. For finite axis-parallel rectangle families, clique constraints equal common-point constraints by the interval Helly property in each coordinate.

If the relaxation optimum is $L$, its gap ratio is $L/\nu$. Exact optimality requires a primal fractional rectangle weighting and a dual point weighting of equal value.

\begin{proposition}[Exact rational improvement]
\[
\frac{17891}{8064}<\frac{73}{32}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The denominators are positive. Cross-multiplication gives
\[
73\cdot8064=588{,}672>572{,}512=17891\cdot32.
\]
The difference is $16{,}160$.
\end{proof}
Numerically the two values are approximately $2.2186$ and $2.28125$. This proves the comparison, not that a particular family has the latter optimum; that conclusion needs matching optimization certificates.

\section{Certificate-checking algorithms}
A triangle-freeness checker enumerates triples. For each triple it computes the maximum lower and minimum upper endpoint in each coordinate. A common point exists exactly when both resulting intervals are nonempty. The algorithm takes $O(n^3)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space.

A disjoint-witness checker tests all $\binom{k}{2}$ pairs of a proposed $k$-member witness, using interval separation in at least one coordinate. It takes $O(k^2)$ time and constant auxiliary space.

A block-slot checker groups rectangles by their label pair and verifies that every pair in a common group intersects. Direct checking takes $O(n^2)$ time; hashing labels first gives expected linear grouping time plus $O(\sum_c n_c^2)$ pair checks over cells $c$. These procedures verify supplied certificates; finding optimal labels, packings, or transversals is a distinct search problem.

\section{Applications and broader interpretation}
Bounded-fiber arguments apply whenever local resource limits control a global selection: scheduling with interference-limited channels, sensor placement with bounded incidence depth, and spatial databases with range objects and query points. The proof pattern is to map objects into finite resources, bound every fiber, and sum capacities.

The recursive law warns that enumeration quickly becomes infeasible: level three already has packing scale $65{,}536$. Symmetry and certificates are therefore essential.

\section{Certificate composition as a design principle}
A general construction workflow begins by choosing a small certificate space $Q$ with capacities $c_q$, then designing geometric gadgets so that every admissible selection maps into $Q$ within those capacities, and finally producing a witness that saturates enough cells to match the upper bound.

For the four-by-four packing scheme,
\[
Q=\{1,2,3,4\}\times\{1,2,3,4\},
\qquad c_q=1.
\]
Equality at size $16$ forces every cell to be occupied. On the piercing side, the certificate space is the chosen transversal $T$, with uniform capacities $c_t=2$. Equality for $64$ members and $32$ points forces every assigned fiber to contain two rectangles.

Product certificate spaces explain composition. If two independent stages have $m$ and $n$ capacity-one cells, their product has $mn$ cells. Composing a level with an identical copy therefore squares the packing bound, giving structural meaning to $a_{r+1}=a_r^2$.

\section{Limitations}
The present theorems do not supply endpoint coordinates, verify triangle-freeness for a concrete family, derive the four-by-four geometric rule, exhibit a $32$-point transversal, or provide primal and dual data for an exact $73/32$ relaxation gap. They provide the exact implication that the first three geometric certificates force $\nu=16$ and $\tau\geq32$.

\section{Future directions}
A compact integer-coordinate realization should reduce every claim to endpoint-order comparisons and should include a $32$-point transversal if exact piercing is sought. Packing slots and piercing points may be two projections of one capacitated incidence matrix, potentially yielding a duality and composition theorem. Equality in the slot bound may force saturation through the recursive composition tree and give a recurrence for the number of maximum packings. Finally, symmetric primal and dual recurrences may produce finite point-relaxation gaps increasing toward $5/2$.

\section{Conclusion}
Point-triangle-freeness turns every transversal point into a bin of capacity two. Ordered block-slot labels turn every admissible packing cell into a bin of capacity one. For sixty-four members, these certificates give $\tau\geq32$ and, with a sixteen-member disjoint witness, $\nu=16$. Since $2\nu-1=31$, they imply a strict violation. The same method yields the recursive scale $4^{2^r}$ and cleanly separates rational gap comparisons from the optimization evidence required to establish an exact optimum.

\end{document}

